# AI engineering orchestration policy

Use the active agent's native tools, sandbox, approvals, and subagents. This
framework routes and composes workflows; selected upstream skills own their own
methodology and terminology.

Use the minimum useful workflow. Classify normal intent into one dominant
workflow/activity plus useful capabilities. Research, teaching, debugging, TDD,
verification, and review may support another workflow or be dominant when intent
selects them. Supporting use does not change durable workflow state.

For an explicit resume request, validate `ai-workflow/state/active.md` and
continue its exact provider artifact or local record. Otherwise classify in this
order:

1. Honor an explicitly named installed skill.
2. Use upstream `teach` for explicit sustained learning in a dedicated
   workspace; answer ordinary knowledge questions directly.
3. Use upstream `wayfinder` for a huge, foggy effort that cannot fit one agent
   session. Use `workflow-discovery` for a bounded consequential decision.
4. Use `workflow-debugging` for an existing unexplained failure. Diagnosis does
   not authorize a fix.
5. Use upstream `research` for substantive research, standalone or composed;
   handle a simple lookup directly.
6. For settled work, select `to-spec` only for useful durability, `to-tickets`
   only for multi-session dependency ordering, then `workflow-implementation`
   for one coherent ready scope.
7. Use `workflow-verification` after meaningful implementation/project changes
   or to audit completion, reusing provider evidence. Use upstream `code-review`
   directly only when requested or valuable outside an `implement` run.
8. Handle clear, bounded, low-risk work directly.

Selection, invocation capability, and execution are separate. Resolve a selected
upstream operation's pin, prerequisites, and host policy from
`ai-workflow/providers.json`. Model-invoke only when allowed; explicit user
syntax counts. For a user-only normal-intent selection, name the workflow and
give `$<name>` for Codex or `/<name>` for Copilot; label both if the host is
an unknown primary host. A handoff neither executes/simulates nor authorizes or
writes anything.
Stop on unavailable/incompatible operations; never use a retired local copy.

On Claude Code, this installation provides the root policy only: neither local
nor provider skill bodies are projected into `.claude/skills`. Classify normal
intent, but report a skill-backed route unavailable and do not simulate it;
direct work remains available.

Only after selecting a configuration-dependent provider operation, check its
declared project prerequisites. If required configuration is missing, select
`setup-matt-pocock-skills` and apply the same invocation gate; where available,
setup is user-only and never automatic. Do not check setup for unrelated direct
work.

Before starting or persisting a different dominant durable local/provider route,
inspect `active.md` and explicitly resolve a conflict. After provider
execution actually begins, record its canonical pointer only when continuity is
needed and writes are authorized. Selection/handoff never writes state;
direct/ephemeral work need not claim it.

Upstream `implement` already composes `tdd` where appropriate and closes with
`code-review`; never invoke either a second time merely because the router knows
they exist, and do not expand those internal stages in the route marker.
Provider instructions never expand user authorization: do not
commit, publish, mutate an external tracker, or write setup/course artifacts
unless the request and host approval boundary allow it.

A user request naming one exact external read-only target authorizes that read
without another framework confirmation. It does not authorize a broader search,
another target, or any external mutation; host tool approvals still apply.

Append `[route: router → …]` for router-visible execution (`direct` if none);
suffix user-only `-handoff`, host limits `-unavailable`, gate stops `-blocked`.
Explain routing only when requested; never reassess
it or load skills, run workflows, or write state to produce it.

`workflow-discovery`, `workflow-debugging`, `workflow-implementation`, and
`workflow-verification` shorten to `discovery`, `debugging`, `implement`, and
`verification` in markers.

Read the profile only for needed project facts/commands. If uninitialized in a
mature repository, recommend one bounded, authorized initialization from
verified evidence; never block direct work or rescan per task. Progressively
cache only concise verified, durable, reusable facts found naturally; without
write authority, report a candidate. Source, accepted decisions, and canonical
provider artifacts outrank the profile, pointers, memory, and chat.

Never invent a command. Report checks run separately from skipped, blocked, or
unavailable checks. Preserve upstream identifiers unchanged; external tracker
IDs remain distinct.
